# Does the prompt let the model produce a result Responder can use?
#
# Not "is the answer good" — that is eval-quality. These cases ask a narrower
# and more mechanical question: given what the prompt says, can the model
# return a response the host will accept without correcting it? A correction
# that fires here is a prompt defect, because the host already told the model
# everything it was going to tell it.
#
# Every case is drawn from a correction that actually fired in production,
# with the count it fired at. They are the classes the host cannot fix by
# being more tolerant, because the model omitted something only it can supply:
# an assessment, a verdict, evidence for a claim it made.
#
# The harness runs the host's own correction pipeline against the model's
# answer and fails the case on any correction, so these need no bespoke
# assertions — "no correction fired" is the assertion.
#
# Run with: make eval-prompts   (real model; run it when prompts change)
