# The one case that is not a Slack turn. At rotation the host asks the session
# it is about to retire for the summary that dies with its transcript, and it
# accepts exactly one shape back: a silent ignore carrying one update_memory
# with something durable in it. There is no correction ladder behind this turn
# and no second attempt, so a prompt that cannot produce that shape first time
# loses the transcript instead of carrying it forward. The input stands in for
# the transcript, which a fresh evaluation session does not have.
{"name": "a rotated session hands its memory forward", "tags": ["prompt-contract", "handoff", "memory", "smoke"], "kind": "handoff", "input": "Session working notes, 34 turns.\n- #backend-ops asked why emisar checkout p99 doubled after the 14:10 rollout.\n- The regression is real: p99 sat at 1.9s from 14:12, against 0.9s across the preceding day.\n- The load balancer is ruled out: the 5xx ratio stayed at 0.000 and no backend health check flapped.\n- Traced to cache warmup: the new revision starts with an empty local cache and the warmup job only runs hourly, so every rollout misses until it fires.\n- The operator chose to watch checkout p99 through the next deploy rather than roll back.\n- Still open: whether the warmup job can be triggered from the deploy pipeline, and who owns it.", "want_action": "ignore", "max_duration_ms": 480000}
